{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014xzDK2T2W1Q34WRzSTej3a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge on edge cases. This project derives specs from C source code, generates Go implementations via AI (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: A four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) per utility. Key components:\n- `cmd/` — one Go package per utility\n- `pkg/sys/` — Darwin/Linux syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` — shared output formatting (tables, colors, units)\n- `pkg/testutils/` — differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` — Mage build targets (boundary between human spec work and automated synthesis)\n- `docs/` — PRDs, use cases, test suites (human-authored, read-only to the pipeline)\n\n**Current state**: Specifications exist for multiple utilities. All code generation shows `codestatus: not started` on the working branch — generated code lives only at `generation-merged` tags from prior pipeline runs. Batches 00.0 through 04.1 have specs at varying levels of completion.\n\n**Current release focus**: Release **04.1** — \"ls extended specification and differential test\" (use case `rel04.1-uc001-ls-extended`). Spec status is `spec_complete`; code generation has not started.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nRelease 04.1 is the assigned scope. It contains one use case: `rel04.1-uc001-ls-extended`. The spec (PRD, use case, test suite) is complete. The next step is code generation.\n\n**Dependencies**: ls extended builds on ls core (rel04.0), which also shows `codestatus: not started`. The orchestrator handles cross-release ordering, so I assume `cmd/ls/` with core functionality exists when rel04.1 tasks execute.\n\n**Task scope**: ls is a high-complexity utility. With a 4-requirement limit and 150–250 line budget, I must pick a focused, coherent slice. Sorting options (-S, -t, -X, -U, -r) and timestamp selection/formatting (-c, -u, --time, --time-style, --full-time) form a natural unit — they're closely related (sorting by time requires knowing which timestamp to use) and self-contained enough for one task.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement ls extended sorting and time display -- rel04.1-uc001-ls-extended\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd009-ls.yaml (ls PRD covering all flags, exit codes, and stream behaviors -- if not at this path, search docs/specs/product-requirements/ for the ls PRD)\n      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel04.1-uc001-ls-extended.yaml (concrete execution path for ls extended features)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-04.1.yaml (test suite for ls extended)\n      - cmd/ls/main.go (existing core ls implementation from rel04.0 -- contains flag parsing, entry reading, and basic output)\n      - pkg/sys/ (file metadata abstractions for stat/lstat, ctime/atime access)\n      - pkg/format/ (output formatting utilities for timestamp display)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/sys and pkg/format component descriptions, DD6 locale constraint)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/ls/sort.go\n        action: create\n        note: Sorting comparators for size, time, extension, directory-order, and reverse\n      - path: cmd/ls/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add sorting and time selection/display flags to CLI parsing and integrate with entry output\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement entry sorting by size (-S), modification time (-t), extension (-X), and directory order (-U/--sort=none) per the ls PRD sorting requirements\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement reverse sort order (-r/--reverse) that inverts any active sort comparator\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement timestamp selection: -c for status change time (ctime), -u for access time (atime), --time=WORD for named time fields, defaulting to modification time\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement --time-style=STYLE (full-iso, long-iso, iso, locale) and --full-time (alias for --time-style=full-iso) for controlling timestamp output format in long listing mode\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use pkg/sys file metadata abstractions for accessing ctime and atime fields portably across Darwin and Linux, per ARCHITECTURE.yaml pkg/sys component (st_mtimespec/st_mtim abstraction)\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use pkg/format for timestamp output formatting in long listing mode, per ARCHITECTURE.yaml pkg/format component\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"All sorting and time display behavior targets LC_ALL=C semantics only, per ARCHITECTURE.yaml design decision DD6\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/ls sorts entries by size when -S is passed, by modification time when -t is passed, by extension when -X is passed, and preserves directory order when -U is passed\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"-r flag reverses the sort order for any active sort mode including the default alphabetical sort\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"-c flag switches displayed and sorted timestamp to ctime; -u switches to atime; default remains mtime\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"--time-style=full-iso, --time-style=long-iso, --time-style=iso, and --time-style=locale each produce correctly formatted timestamp strings matching GNU ls output\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"--full-time is equivalent to --time-style=full-iso and produces the same output\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"go build ./cmd/ls/ compiles without errors and go vet ./cmd/ls/ reports no issues\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21095,"cache_read_input_tokens":14011,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":21095,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ef06ed1f-e885-4548-be28-c23af52fdac4","uuid":"cea41974-7dcc-4bca-bfb9-e3ebe008f108"}
